    "result_text": "All tests pass. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI replaced the direct level-computation rule with a points system, mapped points to a level, and exposed both fields.\n\n**`util/MembershipLevels.java`** \u2014 rewrote the logic:\n- New `pointsFor(Owner)`: starts at 0, `+2` email present, `+1` when `namesakeCount == 0`, `+2` for a household of 3+ (`householdMemberCount >= 3`), `+3` for tenure over 365 days.\n- `levelFor(Owner)` now derives from points: `1` (0\u20131), `2` (2\u20133), `3` (4\u20135), `4` (6+).\n\n**`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a `membershipPoints` mapping (via `pointsFor`) alongside the existing `membershipLevel` mapping, so the response returns both fields.\n\n**`resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `membershipPoints` schema property (drives the generated `OwnerDto`) and updated the `membershipLevel` description to reflect the new derivation.\n\nThe household count is stamped onto the owner before mapping (`HouseholdMembers.stamp`), so the points calc sees the correct household size. Verified against the spec's worked example: a fresh owner with email, no namesake, single-member household, zero tenure \u2192 3 points \u2192 level 2, which is exactly what `AcceptanceTest` asserts.\n\n`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS** (this repo has a single test class, `AcceptanceTest`).",
